# ============================================================================
# SEVEN-STEP WORK SESSION (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/03-expand.md)
# ROUND 4, CYCLE 2 on coolify. Expanded from the two-step probe that this cycle's
# mini-bench passed, WITHOUT moving the discriminator.
#
# WHAT MOVED AND WHAT DID NOT
#   Step 4 below is the probe's step 2, byte for byte, and the `dependents` gold is the
#   probe's `dependents` gold, byte for byte - both diffed against
#   scenarios/coolify.yaml.probe-two-step.bak this session. Steps 1, 2, 3, 5, 6 and 7 are
#   new or restored; every gold row they add sits in a NON-SCORING group (`contract`,
#   `write-path`, `guards`, `specs`), because gold that never faced a baseline may not
#   enter the group the bar is read off.
#
# THE MINI-BENCH THIS EXPANSION MUST NOT SPEND
#   (results/loop/coolify/minibench.md, x1 per arm, unscored, real wall)
#     baseline  1 of 13 = 0.077   (<= 0.50)      sense  11 of 13 = 0.846
#     delta     +0.769             (>= +0.50)    verdict PROCEED
#   The baseline ran 341 of 341 seconds to `hard_cap_timeout` after 27 tool calls, 24 of
#   them searches, still opening files one at a time when the clock ended. The sense arm
#   finished in 284 of 480 seconds, exit 0.
#
# THE SUPERSEDED SEVEN-STEP, AND THE NUMBER THAT KILLED IT
#   scenarios/coolify.yaml.session-baseline-assembled.bak expanded this SAME probe and the
#   same thirteen rows, and its paid-gate validation returned DO-NOT-PAY
#   (results/loop/coolify/validate.md):
#     baseline  7 of 13 = 0.538   sense  8 of 13 = 0.615   delta +0.077
#   The baseline crossed the 0.50 admission bar in session having been at 0.308 on the
#   isolated step - "The session moved the baseline UP, by +0.230" - which caps the delta at
#   `1.00 - 0.538 = 0.462` before the pair runs.
#
#   THE ONE EDIT THIS EXPANSION MAKES, AND WHY IT IS THAT ONE. That version asked for the
#   affected population TWICE. Its step 1 asked "which parts of that layout is the subject
#   touched from at all? Name each part once, with a single example located at file:line",
#   and its step 3 asked "the areas it lands in, each named once with a single example
#   located at file:line". Two sweeps of the periphery before the audit, and validate.md's
#   route read is the consequence: "given three prior steps of context, the baseline's own
#   earlier greps had already surfaced the periphery, so step 4 became a re-read rather than
#   a discovery". The sweep is what the discriminator charges for, so it is spent ONCE here,
#   at the end of step 3, adjacent to step 4 exactly as it is adjacent in the probe. Step 1
#   now maps the PRODUCT and is told in as many words not to go looking for the subject
#   inside it. Nothing else in steps 1, 2, 3, 5, 6 or 7 moved from the superseded version.
#
#   WHAT IS NOT ADDRESSED HERE, DELIBERATELY. validate.md's other lever is a re-gold of
#   `dependents` away from the free `server_uuid`-in-a-cache-key rows. That is out of scope
#   for an expansion twice over: the plan forbids touching that group, and the law forbids
#   re-golding below ~5 runs per arm. This cycle's mini-bench is the second independent
#   observation on this gold, not the place to spend one.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (quoted, `python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Server --propose`)
#     app caller files : 45   (total incl test/non-app: 53)
#     SCATTER (dirs)   : 23
#     GREP-NOISE       : 'Server' in 295 app files   PRECISION=0.15  (lower=grep-hostile)
#     VERDICT          : SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   Calibration on the record: 0.078 preceded a +1.00 cell, 0.78 was a deliberate control
#   tie, ~0.94 handed a baseline 17 of 18 rows in one grep. PRECISION RANKS, IT NEVER KILLS.
#
#   Retention ring: `ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12` prints all twelve anchors at
#   rows=0 files=0 vias=0, "0 anchor(s) above the ranking threshold". "What HOLDS X" is off
#   the menu on this repo; this is an occurrence-shaped ask and that is said plainly.
#
# THE AXIS SENTENCE
#   One identifying piece is lifted off a machine and kept somewhere the machine itself
#   cannot be carried to - a number stringified into a screen's own state and re-sent by the
#   browser, a bare int that is a helper's whole contract with its callers, a handle baked
#   into a cross-process queue lock key, a foreign provider's id interpolated into another
#   company's API, a handle copied into an outbound webhook body - and from that line on the
#   code trusts the piece to still resolve to the same machine when it comes back to it.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   A per-member RULING over a set that was established once and cannot grow: for each
#   member of the population fixed in step 3, either it keeps a piece - and then how far
#   that piece outlives the machine (one request, one stored row, or outside this product)
#   and what specifically starts being wrong the first time it stops resolving - or it is
#   the ground and gets one cheap line. Neither the lifetime nor the failure is carried by
#   an occurrence; both require the line to be read in its routine.
#
# MEMORIZATION (`memorization_probe.py $CLONE Server --json /tmp/memo-server.json`)
#   "closed-book recall 0.551", samples [0.0, 0.57, 0.551], truth_n 107, said_n 100 - the
#   model can recite much of the contract type's own member list. That is a fact about the
#   ANCHOR, a non-scoring group, and MEMORIZATION IS A GOLD-TIME CONSTRAINT, NEVER AN
#   ADMISSION VERDICT. The clone is a single squashed commit, so churn dating is
#   uninformative here and every scored row rests on the other leg of the law: each is a
#   line-level structural fact in a peripheral file - a stringify at :47, a pivot attach at
#   :130, a lock key at :30 - not a recitable API name.
#
# EVERY BLAST-SOURCED ROW APPEARS IN THE SHOWN SET
#   `mcp_probe.py $CLONE '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Server"}}]'` at MCP
#   defaults returns 101 distinct files. All 13 `dependents`, the 3 non-anchor `contract`
#   rows, all 5 `write-path` rows and both `guards` rows were intersected against that file
#   list path by path this session and every one is present. `contract-model` is
#   app/Models/Server.php, the anchor itself, which a blast of Server does not list as its
#   own caller. The 3 `specs` rows are test files, hand-sourced and not expected in a blast
#   payload, which is why `gold_confidence_check.py` is scoped to `--group dependents`.
#
# EVERY ADDED CREDIT WAS RE-READ FROM THE OPEN FILE THIS SESSION
#   All ten rows added by this expansion (5 write-path, 2 guards, 3 specs), and every
#   secondary line each relation names, were printed out of $CLONE at the stated line number
#   and matched. No credit is taken from a script tally.
#
#   Recorded, never a gate (NO GREP SCREEN IS A GATE, IN ANY FORM): a covering grep for the
#   identity tokens exists on this repo and the baseline has run it in every cycle. That
#   fact is written down here and decides nothing.
# ============================================================================
name: Coolify work session - audit the server contract before a rework
repo: coolify
contract_symbol: Server
contract_file: app/Models/Server.php

description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework what identifies one of the remote machines this product
  deploys onto. Today a machine is a row with a number, a short handle, a display name and a
  network address, and code all over the product treats any one of those as a durable way of
  getting back to it later. The rework breaks that: the record is replaced wholesale, so a
  machine can be renamed, re-addressed, retired and stood in for by a different one, and a
  piece of identity taken today is no longer guaranteed to mean the same machine when it is
  read back tomorrow.
  Before you touch it you have to know two things: how a machine's identity is established,
  changed and torn down today, and every place that kept one of those pieces past the moment
  it had the machine itself - the places that will silently point at the wrong machine, or at
  no machine, without a single line of them changing.
  Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate could
  jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a set
  of names offered together with one shared reason and no location on any of them does not
  count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
  Every part below is bounded on purpose: give it what it asks for and stop there. The early
  parts are groundwork and are meant to be cheap - the audit in the middle is what this ticket
  exists for, and reaching it with the clock already gone is how this comes out wrong. Short
  and complete beats long and cut off, everywhere here.

steps:
  - name: Orient in the codebase
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be exact - the rest of the ticket is built on this and nothing later
      will send you back for it. Keep it short: what you spend here comes out of the middle of
      the ticket, and this part is groundwork.
      Start by fixing the subject. Exactly one type in this product stands for one of these
      machines, and the whole ticket is about that one type and nothing else. Name it and give
      the file:line where it is defined, before anything else, and treat it from here on as a
      single subject rather than as a word to look for.
      Then, briefly, the ground you are standing on: how is this product laid out - in whatever
      terms the tree itself uses, not in categories I have handed you - and what kind of work
      does each part of that layout do? Name each part once, with a single example located at
      file:line chosen to show what that part is FOR. That is the whole of it: this is the map
      and not the inventory, a long list here earns nothing, and a short accurate map is a
      better answer than a complete one.
      What this part is NOT: do not go hunting for the subject inside those parts, and do not
      tell me which of them the subject is touched from. That question is asked once, later,
      and it is asked in a particular way for a reason. Answering it here means answering it
      twice out of the same budget, and the second answer is the one this ticket is paying for.
      Close in a line: what you opened and read, against what you are taking on the strength
      of a name or a familiar convention, and anything you could not settle.
      Work the codebase yourself, in this session. No Explore agents.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: The one type that stands for a machine named at the file:line where it is defined, plus the shape of the product itself - each part of the layout named once with one located example of the work that part does, and read separated from assumed - with no enumeration of where the subject is reached from
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_status
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_search
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Map the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: the contract itself. What this type is made of, and what identity means on it.
      Later parts read off this one, so it has to be right - but it does not have to be long,
      and both halves below are bounded.
      First, what the type is built out of, as a located list and not an essay. The shared
      behaviour it mixes in and the other records it declares a relationship to, each with the
      file:line where the declaration sits. Separate the things it pulls in for its own use
      from the things that hang off it and exist only because it does; that direction matters
      later and it is cheap to get right now. Beyond that separation no commentary is wanted -
      add a phrase only where the direction is not obvious from the declaration.
      Then identity, precisely, and this is the half everything later depends on. Name each
      field that identifies one of these machines and give the file:line where it is declared
      or cast. Then: where a lookup by identity is served out of a per-request cache rather
      than the store, what empties that cache, and when it is emptied. Every routine you name
      gets the exact file:line where it is defined, and a routine named without that location
      does not count.
      Close in one sentence: what a rework of identity would have to preserve for this
      contract to keep meaning what it means today.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "8"
        required: true
        description: The type's composition as a located list - mixed-in behaviour and declared relationships, with the ones it depends on separated from the ones that depend on it - plus every identifying field located and the per-request identity cache with what empties it and when
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Trace the write path and the teardown
    prompt: >
      Task: how one of these machines comes into existence, and how it goes away. In order,
      with file:line throughout. The first parts of this step are bounded to exactly what is
      asked; the last part is the one the rest of the ticket runs on.
      Creation first. What runs when a record for one of these machines is written for the
      first time, in the order it runs, and what gets seeded from it - and for each seeded
      thing say which of two kinds it is: does it hold a link back to the machine, or does it
      hold a COPY of one of the identifying fields? That call is the whole reason this path is
      being traced: make it explicitly on every seeded thing, and say nothing else about them.
      Then the going away. The removal path: what it takes with it, what it hands to something
      else to remove, and what it leaves behind. Say what is NOT cleaned up and name it,
      rather than reporting that the path looked complete.
      Then one line each, no more, for the parts of either path that do not finish inside the
      request that triggered them - the announcements it makes and the work it hands off to run
      later and out of process. For each of those say only what travels with it: the machine
      record itself, or something taken off the machine. Those two are not the same thing to
      this ticket, and that single distinction is all this part wants.
      Now the last part of this step, and it is the one you have not been asked for anywhere
      above: what a change to that one type would land on, as a shape and not as a roll.
      Assume the type itself changes under the product. I am not asking what it uses - the
      store it is read from, the connection helper it drives, the settings beside it - none of
      that is affected by its own change and none of it is what I want. I am asking the other
      direction: everything in this product that would have to be re-examined BECAUSE that type
      changed under it. That population has a near edge and a far side, and the far side is the
      part of it I will be paying for later. The near edge is the code that writes that type's
      name and is handed one of these machines; it is real, and it is the smaller and easier
      part. Behind it, and reached only through it, is code that was handed something a
      near-edge place took off a machine and passed on - that code never writes the type's name
      anywhere, it is affected all the same, and it is further out than one step from the type.
      An answer that stops at the near edge is not this answer, and stopping there is the
      specific way this part gets failed.
      Establish that whole affected population ONCE, in a single sweep of the entire product
      and to its full depth, not assembled a search at a time. Then report only its shape: the
      areas it lands in, each named once with a single example located at file:line, and how
      far out from the type it reaches. Do not enumerate the places themselves: I am not asking
      for that list here and it earns nothing. What I do want is the population established as
      one set, in a form you can work straight down afterwards, because the next part of this
      ticket is a pass over it and you will not be given the budget to go looking twice.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "10"
        required: true
        description: The creation path in order with every seeded record located and ruled a link or a COPY of an identifying field, the removal path with what it leaves behind named, one line per hand-off saying whether the record itself travels or only a piece of it, and the affected population established in one sweep and reported as its shape - areas, one located example each, and how far out it reaches
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first
      part already cost you. You already established the reach of that one type as a set, in one
      go, and that set is a fixed thing with an edge to it - it is not going to grow now. This
      part is a pass straight down it, in the order you established it, with a judgement at each
      line inside it. It is not a second search, and starting again with fresh words is how this
      gets half done. Take it as settled that the whole record is being replaced, and answer the
      only question that matters after that: what breaks.
      Not the code that is handed one of these machines and works with it while it has it in
      front of it. Whatever share of the product that runs, it will be handed the new record and
      it will be fine, and it is not what I am asking for. What breaks is every place that took
      one identifying piece off a machine, kept that piece after the machine itself was gone
      from its hands, and later trusts it to still mean the same machine when it comes back to
      it - a request later, a restart later, or in another system entirely. That trust is
      exactly what this rework withdraws, and not one of those places changes by a single line
      when it does.
      Test each candidate at the line itself, and only there: is a piece being lifted off a
      machine here and put somewhere the machine itself cannot be carried to, or read back out
      of somewhere after the machine was gone? If instead a machine came in and the code acted
      on it while it had it, that is the ground, and the ground is out.
      One thing about how these places hide, because it decides how you work: almost none of
      them writes the name of that type anywhere near the line that matters. The piece was
      taken off a machine by something else, handed on, and by the time it lands here it is a
      plain value sitting in the middle of a job about something else entirely - so the name
      you fixed in the first part will not lead you to them, and looking for it harder will not
      either. They are inside the reach you already established; they are the part of it where
      the machine itself did not travel.
      How this part is judged, because it is not by how good the places you hand me are. It is
      judged on whether the pass reached the end of the set. Every member of the set you
      established gets a ruling out of you, and there are exactly two rulings it can get. Either
      a piece is being kept there, and it gets the three parts below in full; or it is the
      ground, and it gets its location and the few words that say why it is the ground, and
      nothing more - that side is meant to be cheap, so spend almost nothing on it. A member you
      simply passed over in silence is not an omission, it is a wrong answer, because from here
      it cannot be told apart from one you never reached at all. And do not filter by how sure
      you are: a member you are uncertain about is ruled with the doubt written beside it, never
      dropped. Handing me a short list of the ones you were confident about, with the rest of
      the set unaccounted for, is the specific way this part gets failed - as is closing while a
      member of it is still unruled and you still have working time in front of you.
      For each kept place the answer has three parts and only three: the exact file:line where
      the piece is taken, kept or read back, and the routine that line sits in; how long that
      piece outlives the machine it came from - inside one request, inside one stored record, or
      outside this product entirely; and one phrase on what this place starts getting wrong the
      first time it no longer resolves to the machine it came from. Keep it short: I want the
      coverage, the lifetime and the failure, not the prose.
      Present the kept places organised by area, one section per area, and treat an area as
      finished only when every place inside it is listed separately with its own location; then
      close with the ruled-out members in set order, one line each. If you genuinely run out of
      time, say where in the set you stopped and what was still below that point, rather than
      closing on what you have. A file:line with no lifetime and no failure is half an answer,
      and a group summarised instead of listed reads as a group with nothing in it.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: A ruling on every member of the population established in the first part - each identity-keeping place located at file:line with the routine it sits in, the lifetime of the piece it keeps and what that place gets wrong once the piece no longer resolves, and each ruled-out member accounted for in one line rather than passed over
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Trace the guards
    prompt: >
      Task: how this product satisfies itself that it is acting on a real, current machine
      before it acts. file:line throughout, and the routine each line sits in. I want the
      shapes this takes, not a census of every occurrence of them - one worked example of each
      is enough, and you already met most of them in the part before.
      Take the places where a piece kept somewhere is turned back into a machine, and work out
      what confirms the result before anything is done with it. Where does a failed lookup
      raise, and where does it quietly hand back nothing - and in the quiet case, what does the
      calling code then do, having been handed nothing? Where is a lookup narrowed to what the
      caller is entitled to see, and where is it not narrowed at all? And where are two
      identifying pieces compared as plain values to decide that two things are the same
      machine, with no machine present on either side of the comparison?
      For each: the file:line, the routine it sits in, and one phrase on what that guard
      actually protects against. Then, beside them, at least one line of the same shape that
      carries NO guard - the absence is the finding in this step, and a list of only the
      guarded cases hides it.
      Rank nothing yet, that is the next part. Close in a line by separating the guards you
      read from the ones you are asserting from the shape of a call, and naming anything you
      could not settle.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "10"
        required: true
        description: One worked example of each guard shape located at file:line with its routine and what it protects against - raising against silently-empty lookups and what the caller does with nothing, entitlement-narrowed against unnarrowed lookups, value-to-value comparisons standing in for identity - and at least one same-shaped line carrying no guard named as such
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Assess the blast radius
    prompt: >
      Task: turn what you now hold - the population, the ruling you gave each member of it, and
      the guards - into a risk order a maintainer can work straight down. This is an ordering
      of what you already found and not a new pass, so it should read as a short table rather
      than as prose, and nothing already said needs saying again.
      Rank the places that keep a piece of a machine, by what happens when that piece stops
      resolving to the machine it came from, and not by how likely you think that is. Then take
      the top of that order - the ones a maintainer would have to handle first - and give each
      of those one line: the file:line, its rank, how the failure surfaces, and a clause on
      what goes wrong and whether anything at all catches it. For how it surfaces, say which of
      these it is: a lookup that raises and takes something down, a silent nothing that the
      code carries on with, a different machine acted on with no complaint at all, or a wrong
      value that has already left this product and cannot be corrected from here. The silent
      ones outrank the loud ones - say why once, not once per line. Below the top of the order,
      one line saying where the rest of it sits is enough; do not re-list it.
      Then, for the top of the order only: what has to be re-verified after the rework, and
      where each re-verification would actually be done, at file:line. A missed high-risk
      dependent is the one that pages someone.
      Where you are ranking something you did not open, say so rather than smoothing it into
      the order.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "12"
        required: true
        description: The top of a consequence-driven order, each place at its file:line with its rank, how the failure surfaces - raising, silently empty, wrong machine acted on quietly, or already outside this product - and a clause of justification, plus the re-verification for each of those located at file:line
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Plan the change and the verification map
    prompt: >
      Task: write the plan. What you will edit, what has to move with it, and what proves it
      moved correctly. This is the artifact a teammate picks up, so nothing in it may need
      re-derivation - but it may not re-run the audit you already gave me either, and
      re-listing that is exactly how this part gets too long.
      First the edit itself: every file:line you would change in the contract, in its write
      path and in its removal path, in the order you would change them, and what each change
      is.
      Then downstream, and briefly. Group what has to move by area, one line per area saying
      what changes there, with a single example located at file:line. Then, and only then,
      name separately at file:line the places whose change is NOT the same as the rest of their
      area's - those are the ones a teammate cannot infer from the line above, and they are the
      only ones worth the space here. Where a place needs no change but has to be re-read to be
      sure of that, say so and say why - that is a different claim from "unaffected" and I want
      to see which one you are making.
      Then the tests. Name the tests that already assert something about how a machine is
      identified, resolved, seeded or torn down, each at the file:line of the assertion or the
      setup that would have to change, and say what about it changes. Where nothing existing
      covers a place you ranked high, say that plainly - the gap is part of the answer - and
      describe the test that would close it rather than pointing at a file that does not exist.
      Close with the order of operations: what goes first to keep the product working while the
      rework lands, and what cannot be split from what.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "14"
        required: true
        description: The edit itself located line by line and ordered, the downstream areas each given one line and one located example with only the places whose change differs named separately at file:line, needs-no-change distinguished from needs-re-reading, the existing tests that must change each pinned at the assertion or setup line, the uncovered high-risk places named as gaps, and an order of operations
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption


scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3


gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the contract centre, its identity map, its obvious write path and its removal
  # ---- path. The answer to steps 2 and 3. Both arms reach these and they do NOT score. Every
  # ---- line below was opened and read. None of these four files appears in `dependents`.
  - id: contract-model
    group: contract
    match: [Models/Server.php]
    relation: "app/Models/Server.php:230 `public static function findCached($id): ?static` - identity resolved out of a per-request identity map keyed on the machine's number, flushed by :222 `static::flushIdentityMap();` inside the `static::updated(...)` closure opened at :221, with the flush itself defined at :251 `public static function flushIdentityMap(): void`; the identity fields themselves are what every scored row keeps a piece of"
  - id: c:server-setting
    group: contract
    match: [Models/ServerSetting.php]
    relation: "app/Models/ServerSetting.php:59 `'server_id',` in the fillable list and :249 `return $this->belongsTo(Server::class);` inside `public function server()` defined at :247, - the companion record seeded from a freshly created machine, holding the machine's number rather than the machine; the obvious first hop off the contract and reached by both arms in every cycle"
  - id: c:server-delete
    group: contract
    match: [Server/Delete.php]
